1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,200
Well, most people think that lions only come from Africa.

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:06,839
And you would be forgiven for thinking this,

3
00:00:06,839 --> 00:00:09,620
because in fact most lions do come from Africa.

4
00:00:09,620 --> 00:00:12,230
But this hasn't always been the case.

5
00:00:12,230 --> 00:00:16,851
If we go back ten thousand years we would find that there were lions

6
00:00:16,851 --> 00:00:18,820
roaming vast sections of the globe.

7
00:00:18,820 --> 00:00:25,230
But now, unfortunately, only very small sections of the lions' former habitat remain.

8
00:00:25,230 --> 00:00:29,528
My particular interest is Asiatic lions,

9
00:00:29,528 --> 00:00:32,400
which are a sub-species of African lions.

10
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:36,609
It's almost a hundred thousand years since the Asiatic lions split off

11
00:00:36,609 --> 00:00:38,280
and developed as a sub-species.

12
00:00:38,280 --> 00:00:42,876
At one time the Asiatic lion was living as far west as Greece

13
00:00:42,876 --> 00:00:46,830
and they were found from there, in a band that spread east through various

14
00:00:46,830 --> 00:00:49,600
countries of the Middle East, all the way to India.

15
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:55,284
In museums, you can now see Greek coins that have clear imagines of the

16
00:00:55,284 --> 00:00:56,750
Asiatic lion on them.

17
00:00:56,750 --> 00:01:00,400
Most of them are dated at around 500 B.C.

18
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:06,240
However, Europe saw its last Asiatic lion roaming free two thousand years ago.

19
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:11,237
Over the next nineteen hundred years the numbers of Asiatic lions in the other

20
00:01:11,237 --> 00:01:12,802
areas declined steadily,

21
00:01:12,802 --> 00:01:17,518
but it was only in the nineteenth century that they disappeared from everywhere

22
00:01:17,518 --> 00:01:18,120
but India.

23
00:01:18,120 --> 00:01:24,004
So, how can you tell an Asiatic lion from an African lion, with which

24
00:01:24,004 --> 00:01:25,430
you're probably more familiar?

25
00:01:25,430 --> 00:01:31,920
Well, in general, Asiatic lions are not as big as African lions.

26
00:01:31,920 --> 00:01:34,289
The color is more or less the same,

27
00:01:34,289 --> 00:01:38,747
but the appearance of the mane is different - that's the hair around the lion's face

28
00:01:38,747 --> 00:01:39,510
and neck.

29
00:01:39,510 --> 00:01:44,800
The Asiatic lion's mane is noticeably shorter than the African lion's.

30
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:50,591
Asiatic lions also have a long fold of skin on their undersides whereas not

31
00:01:50,591 --> 00:01:52,320
many African lions have this.

32
00:01:52,320 --> 00:01:57,470
Well, I'd like to talk to you now about the Gir Sanctuary in India.

33
00:01:57,470 --> 00:01:59,560
That's where I've just come back from.

34
00:01:59,560 --> 00:02:04,600
The sanctuary was established specifically to protect the Asiatic lion.

35
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:09,950
It's 1,450 square kilometres in area and most of it is forest.

36
00:02:09,950 --> 00:02:14,348
There are now around three hundred Asiatic lions in India

37
00:02:14,348 --> 00:02:17,140
and almost all of them are in this sanctuary.

38
00:02:17,140 --> 00:02:20,536
But despite living in a sanctuary,

39
00:02:20,536 --> 00:02:24,735
which makes them safe from hunters, they still face a number of problems that

40
00:02:24,735 --> 00:02:25,800
threaten their survival.

41
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:29,550
One of these is the ever-present danger of disease.

42
00:02:29,550 --> 00:02:34,525
This is what killed more than a third of Africa's Serengeti lions in 1994,

43
00:02:34,525 --> 00:02:39,841
and people are fearful that something similar could happen in the Gir Sanctuary

44
00:02:39,841 --> 00:02:42,650
and kill off many of the Asiatic lions there.

45
00:02:42,650 --> 00:02:49,140
India's lions are particularly vulnerable because they have a limited gene pool.

46
00:02:49,140 --> 00:02:55,303
The reason for this is interesting-it's because all of them are descended from a

47
00:02:55,303 --> 00:02:59,479
few dozen lions that were saved by a prince who took a particular interest

48
00:02:59,479 --> 00:02:59,800
in them.

49
00:02:59,800 --> 00:03:01,849
He was very wealthy,

50
00:03:01,849 --> 00:03:06,650
and he managed to protect them-otherwise they'd probably have died out completely.

51
00:03:06,650 --> 00:03:13,000
When you see the Asiatic lion in India, what you sense is enormous vitality.

52
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:18,215
They're very impressive beasts and you would never guess that they had this vulnerability

53
00:03:18,215 --> 00:03:18,940
when you look at them.

54
00:03:18,940 --> 00:03:24,150
The Asiatic lions don't have the Gir Sanctuary to themselves, I should add.

55
00:03:24,150 --> 00:03:28,110
They actually share it with about two thousand farmers.

56
00:03:28,110 --> 00:03:35,112
A significant proportion of the lions' diet is made up of the livestock of these farmers-goats, chickens

57
00:03:35,112 --> 00:03:37,600
and so on - as much as a third, in fact.

58
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:42,650
And they've even been known to attack humans, especially in times of drought.

59
00:03:42,650 --> 00:03:50,213
One final piece of interesting information-in ancient India one of the greatest tests of

60
00:03:50,213 --> 00:03:52,420
leadership for a man was to fight a lion.

61
00:03:52,420 --> 00:03:57,231
Now it seems, in modern India it will be a great test to see

62
00:03:57,231 --> 00:03:58,620
if the lion can be saved.

63
00:03:58,620 --> 00:04:03,230
I'm sure this is something that all of you will share concern for too.
